.TH INPUT 3crunch "" "Crunch" "Crunch Library Manual"
.SH NAME
input \- read a bounded line from standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int input(char " "buf" "[], int " "max_length" ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR input ()
reads bytes from standard input until a newline (LF) or end of file.
It stores at most
.I max_length
bytes in
.I buf
and appends a terminating NUL byte.
The newline is consumed but not stored.
If the line is longer than
.IR max_length ,
the remaining bytes are discarded through the newline or end of file.
If the call succeeds, the next call therefore reads the next line, not
the discarded remainder.
.PP
The caller must provide a writable buffer of at least
.I max_length + 1
bytes.
For example,
.B input(buf, 5)
requires room for six bytes: five input bytes and the terminating NUL.
The function does not allocate or resize the buffer and cannot check its capacity.
.PP
The limit and return count measure bytes, not Unicode characters.
Only LF is removed; a preceding carriage return is retained.
Embedded NUL bytes count toward the result, but ordinary string operations
will stop at the first NUL.
.PP
When
.I max_length
is zero, the function consumes the line and stores only a terminating NUL.
A null buffer or negative limit is rejected without consuming input.
Reads interrupted by a signal are retried.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success,
.BR input ()
returns the number of bytes stored, excluding the terminating NUL.
A final line without a newline is returned normally.
Zero means an empty line, end of file before any bytes, or a zero limit;
these cases are not distinguished.
.PP
On error, the function returns \-1 and sets
.BR errno .
After a read error, any stored prefix is NUL\-terminated, but its length
is not returned.
Bytes already consumed, including any discarded bytes, cannot be recovered.
If the error occurs before the newline, the next call starts at the remaining
part of the same line; the caller must decide how to handle it.
Invalid arguments do not modify the buffer.
Successful calls, including end of file and truncated lines, leave
.B errno
unchanged.
.SH ERRORS
Include
.B <errno.h>
to inspect
.BR errno .
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I buf
is null or
.I max_length
is negative.
.TP
.B EBADF
Standard input is not a valid descriptor open for reading.
.TP
.B EAGAIN
Standard input is nonblocking and no data is available.
.PP
Other errors reported by the Linux
.BR read (3crunch),
.BR read (2)
system call are also possible.
.SH EXAMPLE
Save the following Crunch program as
.BR greeting.bc :
.PP
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <errno.h>

int main() {
    char name[6];
    printf("Name (up to 5 bytes): ");
    int count = input(name, 5);
    if (count < 0) {
        printf("input failed: errno=%d\\n", errno);
        return 1;
    }
    printf("Hello, %s! Read %d bytes.\\n", name, count);
    return 0;
}
.fi
.PP
Compile and run:
.PP
.nf
crunch greeting.bc \-o greeting
\&./greeting
.fi
.SH NOTES
This is a Crunch library function, not an ISO C function.
The Crunch driver links the required implementation from
.B libcrunch.a
automatically.
.B errno
is process\-wide, not thread\-local.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR crunch (1),
.BR read (2)
